---
import GuideArticlePage from '../../components/templates/GuideArticlePage.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why is Rolex service so expensive?',
    a: 'Brand service centers invest in facilities, training, proprietary parts channels, and warranty policies — those structures cost money, and pricing reflects full factory process rather than independent bench economics.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a Rolex service take?',
    a: 'Authorized timelines often stretch multiple weeks to months depending on parts and queue; independent timelines vary with parts access and movement complexity — ask for a window at approval, not at drop-off.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it cheaper to service a Rolex at an independent watchmaker?',
    a: 'Often yes — many independents quote roughly $300–$700 for mechanical service compared with commonly cited authorized totals around $800–$1,500+, but the correct choice depends on warranty needs and cosmetic standards.',
  },
];
---

<GuideArticlePage
  title="Rolex Service Cost Guide | Shelton Watch Services"
  description="Rolex service cost ranges — authorized vs. independent pricing, what moves the number, and how to think about value vs. warranty."
  h1="How Much Does a Rolex Service Cost?"
  answer="Authorized Rolex service frequently totals roughly $800–$1,500+ depending on model and cosmetic work, while many independent specialists land closer to roughly $300–$700 for comparable mechanical servicing — final numbers always depend on parts, movement generation, and case/bracelet refinishing choices."
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHref="/service/rolex/"
  ctaLabel="Book Rolex service →"
>
  <p>
    Price is a symptom of process. Factory service bundles standardized testing, lubrication regimes, and
    accountability — and you pay for that ecosystem. Independents can often be leaner: less overhead, more
    flexible cosmetic decisions, and timelines tied to a single bench. Roughly <strong>3 in 4</strong>{' '}
    post-warranty conversations we see come down to “warranty vs. velocity vs. cash outlay.”
  </p>
  <h2>What Moves Rolex Service Pricing the Most?</h2>
  <p>
    Movement family, parts replacements, chronograph complications, bracelet stretch repair, and
    refinishing depth all swing estimates. A simple three-hander with healthy amplitude behaves differently
    than a movement that has run dry for years — metal wear has a bill attached.
  </p>
  <h2>When Does Authorized Service Make Financial Sense?</h2>
  <p>
    Factory service can protect resale narratives for buyers who care about service card aesthetics — and
    it is the path to manufacturer warranty on that work. If you plan a private sale soon, ask what
    purchasers in your segment actually reward versus what they merely claim to reward.
  </p>
  <h2>How Do You Avoid Sticker Shock?</h2>
  <p>
    Ask for line-item approvals: movement work, gaskets, crystal, hands, dial handling, and cosmetic
    refinishing should be separable decisions whenever possible. For interval planning, pair this article
    with <a href="/guide/how-often-service-rolex/">how often to service a Rolex</a> — and if you are
    weighing liquidation instead, review <a href="/sell/rolex/">selling your Rolex</a>.
  </p>
</GuideArticlePage>
